{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1778517791359,"sessionID":"ses_1e818387dffedBCC6S3FhFEbCH","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"grep","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-96382ed71af7f03a","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"pattern":"mature T cell"},"output":"Found 295 matches (showing first 100)\n/home/runner/work/eval-ont-agent-cl/eval-ont-agent-cl/__issue_context__.json:\n  Line 2:   \"body\": \"The textual and logical definition for this term limits IELs to \\\"intestinal epithelium\\\" only, but IELs are found in multiple epithelial tissues - Gastrointestinal tract, Reproductive tract, oral cavity etc... \\n\\n1) Broaden the definition of \\\"intraepithelial lymphocyte\\\"\\n2) Add a specific subclass \\\"intestinal intraepithelial lymphocyte\\\"\\n\\n**Improved textual definition**\\nA tissue-resident lymphocyte located within the epithelial layer of mucosal tissues, particularly within the gastrointestinal, respiratory, and reproductive tracts. Characterised by permanent residency, this cell typically expresses CD103 (ntegrin alpha-E), which binds to E-cadherin on epithelial cells, enabling epithelial retention in both mice and humans. Phenotypically, IEL displays an activated, antigen-experienced state characterised by the constitutive expression of cytotoxic molecules (granzyme B, perforin) and innate-like receptors (such as NKG2D), enabling rapid, localised surveillance and immediate response to epithelial stress.\\n\\n**Add these references (DO NOT replace existing ones)**\\n\\nPMID: [29674648](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/29674648/)\\nWIKIPEDIA:Intraepithelial lymphocyte\\n\\n**Logical definition**\\n'mature T cell'\\n and ('located in' some 'epithelium')\\n and ('capable of' some 'mucosal immune response')\\n\\n--- \\n\\n**intestinal intraepithelial lymphocyte**\\n\\n**textual definition**\\nA T cell that is located in the intestinal epithelium and is capable of a mucosal immune response.\\n\\nPMID: [29674648](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/29674648/)\\nWIKIPEDIA:Intraepithelial lymphocyte\\n\\n**logical definition**\\n\\nEquivilantTo\\n'mature T cell'\\n and ('located in' some 'intestinal epithelium')\\n and ('capable of' some 'mucosal immune response')\\n\\n**ORCID**\\nhttps://orcid.org/0009-0000-8480-9277\\n\\n\",\n\n\n/home/runner/work/eval-ont-agent-cl/eval-ont-agent-cl/__pr_result__/src/templates/blood_and_immune_upper_slim.csv:\n  Line 34: CL:0002420,http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/cl#blood_and_immune_upper_slim,immature T cell\r\n\n\n/home/runner/work/eval-ont-agent-cl/eval-ont-agent-cl/__pr_result__/src/patterns/data/matches/cellPartOfAnatomicalEntity.tsv:\n  Line 428: http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CL_0009023\tsmall intestine Peyer's patch T cell\thttp://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/UBERON_0003454\tsmall intestine Peyer's patch\thttp://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CL_0002419\tmature T cell\r\n\n\n/home/runner/work/eval-ont-agent-cl/eval-ont-agent-cl/__pr_result__/src/patterns/data/matches/cellCapableOfBiologicalProcess.tsv:\n  Line 59: http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CL_0000815\tregulatory T cell\thttp://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/GO_0050777\tnegative regulation of immune response\thttp://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CL_0002419\tmature T cell\r\n\n\n/home/runner/work/eval-ont-agent-cl/eval-ont-agent-cl/__pr_result__/src/patterns/definitions.owl:\n  Line 996: Maturation of alpha-beta T cells takes place in the thymus and is tightly controlled by various transcriptional and epigenetic regulators. They develop through several stages from progenitors to mature T cells, from CD4-CD8- double negative cells to CD4+CD8+ double positive cells, until they eventually become CD4+ or CD8+ single positive cells.\n\n  Line 1442: cTECs also play a significant part in the selection and development of T cells. During T cell maturation, these cells express both self and non-self proteins via the major histocompatibility complex (MHC) to immature T cells. cTECs then stimulate the T cells that can recognize such proteins, an important event known as positive selection. This process aids in the creation of a diverse T cell receptor (TCR) repertoire that can react to a wide range of antigens, therefore ensuring effective immunity. \n\n  Line 1449: mTECs themselves have a self-renewing ability and sustain the continuous export of mature T cells. They also express a number of genes that encode for chemokines and other signaling molecules which draw in and retain developing T cells for the process of negative selection. The three-dimensional network formed by these cells provides a physical platform for such selection. Together, mTECs hold a critical place in the maintenance of immune homeostasis, demonstrating the conceptual links that exist between population of stromal cells, thymocytes development, tissue-restricted antigen expression, and negative selection.\n\n\n/home/runner/work/eval-ont-agent-cl/eval-ont-agent-cl/__pr_result__/src/patterns/data/default/ExtendedDescription.tsv:\n  Line 1066: cTECs also play a significant part in the selection and development of T cells. During T cell maturation, these cells express both self and non-self proteins via the major histocompatibility complex (MHC) to immature T cells. cTECs then stimulate the T cells that can recognize such proteins, an important event known as positive selection. This process aids in the creation of a diverse T cell receptor (TCR) repertoire that can react to a wide range of antigens, therefore ensuring effective immunity. \n\n  Line 1075: mTECs themselves have a self-renewing ability and sustain the continuous export of mature T cells. They also express a number of genes that encode for chemokines and other signaling molecules which draw in and retain developing T cells for the process of negative selection. The three-dimensional network formed by these cells provides a physical platform for such selection. Together, mTECs hold a critical place in the maintenance of immune homeostasis, demonstrating the conceptual links that exist between population o